    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d727">

                <pb facs="1305" n="1289" sameAs="frus:frus1950v04/pg1289"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.131/5–1350</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Under
                        Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Webb</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-05-13">May 13, 1950</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Jim</hi>: I have been trying to figure out some
                    way to defeat the Soviets in their unilateral stand on the ruble-rate. We here
                    in <pb facs="1306" n="1290" xml:id="pg1290"/> Moscow cannot get rubles except
                    through the Disbursing Officer, who must draw on the Soviet State Bank, using
                    “U.S. Treasury checks. The Treasury is now forced to accept six rubles for one
                    dollar, and on 1 July 1950 must accept only four rubles for one dollar. The
                    Soviets could always change again whenever they liked—say to 2 to 1:</p>
                <p>We need a lot of rubles to run the Embassy, and we all here need rubles for our
                    housekeeping and personal expenses. It is tough now, and will be serious by 1
                    July.</p>
                <p>I have made one suggestion to the Department, namely, that we might accept a
                    flock of rubles in addition to dollars, in settlement of the Lend-Lease Account
                    owed us. There are many angles to such a settlement, but we might get somewhere
                    with the Soviets, if we took a good slice of rubles. It would be necessary to
                    have an understanding with our Treasury that such rubles were to be made
                    available to the Department to defray costs here—and many angles to that,
                    too.</p>
                <p>Whatever scheme, if any, works out, I would strongly recommend we very selfishly
                    insist it be reserved solely for the U.S. We ought not to get soft and intimate
                    we would help out other friendly nations stuck with this same problem.</p>
                <p>This suggestion may not stand up at all, but I mention it as evidence of my
                    concern, and of our soul-searching over here.</p>
                <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Alan</hi> G. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
